  The largest structural problem is that this file mixes the v2 plan, the shipped state, and the corrections. A reader  
  cannot tell which sentences are still design intent. I would split it into a current architecture doc and a short     
  history doc. If that is too much, at least mark each section as "as built" or "as planned."[27C
[120C
  [1mSchema[22m [1mreview[22m[K[105C
[120C
  [1mGood[22m [1mchoices[22m[K[106C
[120C
  - Text ULIDs avoid the [38;2;177;185;249mAUTOINCREMENT [mversus [38;2;177;185;249mSERIAL [msplit, and they sort by time.[K[38C
  - [38;2;177;185;249mCHECK [mconstraints stand in for enums and keep the DDL portable.[K[53C
  - [38;2;177;185;249mUNIQUE (task_id, dedup_key) [mwith a nullable key is correct, and the comment explains why NULL beats an empty string.
  - [38;2;177;185;249mdecided_by [mand [38;2;177;185;249mrule_created [mmake the audit trail distinguish a human from a rule.[K[35C
  - [38;2;177;185;249mdetails [mon a permission stores what the tool would do, which is what an approver needs.[K[29C
[120C
  [1mConcerns[22m[K[110C
[120C
  - [1mTimestamp[22m [1mordering[22m [1mdepends[22m [1mon[22m [1mformat[22m [1mdiscipline.[22m RFC3339 text sorts correctly only if every value is UTC with the   
    same suffix and the same fractional precision. Mixing [38;2;177;185;249mZ [mwith an offset, or [38;2;177;185;249m.5Z [mwith [38;2;177;185;249m.500Z[m, breaks [38;2;177;185;249mORDER BY[K[10C[m
    [38;2;177;185;249mwaiting_since[m. The doc should state the exact format and enforce it in one helper.[K[34C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mrank [m[1mas[22m [1ma[22m [1mfloat[22m [1mhas[22m [1ma[22m [1mprecision[22m [1mceiling.[22m Repeated midpoint inserts at the same spot exhaust a double after roughly  
    50 halvings. The doc mentions on-demand renormalization but not what triggers it. Detect a gap below some epsilon at
    insert time and renormalize the column then.[72C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mwaiting_since [m[1mis[22m [1mdenormalized.[22m It must be set on entry to a waiting state and cleared on exit, in the same[K[10C
    transaction as the status change. A stale value would corrupt the Stack, which is the main "who is blocked longest" 
    answer. The doc should say who owns that invariant. It also should say what happens when a card moves from[10C
    [38;2;177;185;249mneeds-input [mto [38;2;177;185;249mneeds-permission[m: does the clock reset?[K[62C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mUNIQUE[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m(wire_name) [m[1mand[22m [1mreuse.[22m After a task dies, its [38;2;177;185;249mwire_name [mstill blocks a new task with the same name. The      
    identity section says match by [38;2;177;185;249mwire_name[m, so a dead card likely gets revived. That is probably intended, but it     
    should be written down.[93C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mON[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mDELETE[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mCASCADE [m[1mon[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mevent [m[1mand[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mpermission[m[1m.[22m [38;2;177;185;249mDELETE /v1/tasks/{id} [mand [38;2;177;185;249mprune [merase the audit trail together with the  
    card. That contradicts goal 3 ("history can be read back") for exactly the cards the operator forgot. Consider soft 
    delete, or an explicit statement that forgetting is destructive.[52C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mpayload [m[1mand[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1moverrides [m[1mas[22m [1mTEXT.[22m This is fine for portability, but nothing validates that they are JSON. A [38;2;177;185;249mCHECK      [m
    [38;2;177;185;249m(json_valid(...)) [mworks on SQLite and has a Postgres analogue.[K[54C
  - [1mNo[22m [1mretention[22m [1mstory[22m [1mfor[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mevent[m[1m.[22m It is append-only and unbounded. The [38;2;177;185;249moutput [mkind, if used, would grow quickly. A      
    single-user tool tolerates this for a long time, but the doc should say so.[41C
  - [1mIndexes.[22m [38;2;177;185;249mpermission_pending (decided_at, requested_at) [mserves the pending queue. Nothing indexes [38;2;177;185;249mtask.status [mor     
    [38;2;177;185;249mtask.waiting_since[m, and the Stack query orders by [38;2;177;185;249mwaiting_since[m. At single-user scale this is harmless.[K[13C
[120C
  [1mLifecycle[22m [1mrules[22m [1mfor[22m [1mwaiting[22m [1mcards[22m[K[85C
[120C
  The three rules are the most thoughtful part of the domain model, and the reasoning for each is clear. My questions   
  are about edges.[102C
[120C
  - [1mRule[22m [1m1[22m [1m(moving[22m [1mout[22m [1mof[22m [1ma[22m [1mwaiting[22m [1mstate[22m [1mblocks[22m [1mpending[22m [1mrequests).[22m This is correct. Two gaps:[K[26C
    - The doc says the operator is asked first, but it also says "nothing that changes a status can opt out." An API    
      caller has no dialog. The confirmation must live in the client, and the server must apply the block regardless.   
      Say so explicitly.[96C
    - Bulk operations such as [38;2;177;185;249mprune [mand [38;2;177;185;249mkill [mchange status too. Each should go through the same path, or the rule has a 
      hole.[109C
  - [1mRule[22m [1m2[22m [1m(shelved[22m [1mis[22m [1ma[22m [1mstanding[22m [1mno).[22m Sound. The alternative is an agent frozen behind an invisible card. One[K[10C
    consequence to state: a shelved card that receives a blocked request presumably logs [38;2;177;185;249mperm-decided [mwith [38;2;177;185;249mdecided_by   [m
    set to something. Name it, so the log stays distinguishable from a human "block."[35C
  - [1mRule[22m [1m3[22m [1m(nagging).[22m The schedule (every minute for ten, then every five) is reasonable. Open questions:[K[15C
    - What stops the nag when the operator is deliberately away? The doc gives a rule for widening but none for[9C
      silencing.[104C
    - Does the nag survive a daemon restart? Schedule state derived from [38;2;177;185;249mrequested_at [mwould survive. In-memory timers   
      would not.[104C
    - Is there a per-request or global mute, given the "assault" the doc wants to avoid?[32C
[120C
  The one design tension: rule 3 assumes the operator will eventually answer, while rule 1 punishes tidying. Together   
  they push the operator to answer stale requests instead of discarding them. A one-click "block all pending on this    
  card with a reason" would resolve that, and the doc does not mention it.[46C
[120C
  [1mAPI[22m [1msurface[22m[K[107C
[120C
  [1mStrengths[22m[K[109C
[120C
  - The agent-facing and human-facing listeners are separate, so the agent side can shut down while the human side      
    explains why.[103C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m/gate [mmakes [38;2;177;185;249mjoin [mand [38;2;177;185;249mleave [meffective on the next tool call.[K[57C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m/activity [mputs its failure posture on the caller: one second, ignore everything, nobody reads the result.[K[11C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m/session [mrides harness hooks instead of the model, so registration costs no turn.[K[35C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m/v1/browse [mcorrectly lists the daemon's filesystem, not the browser's.[K[46C
[120C
  [1mConcerns[22m[K[110C
[120C
  - [1mNo[22m [1mversioning[22m [1mon[22m [1mthe[22m [1magent-facing[22m [1mendpoints.[22m [38;2;177;185;249m/session[m, [38;2;177;185;249m/gate[m, [38;2;177;185;249m/stop[m, and [38;2;177;185;249m/activity [mhave no [38;2;177;185;249m/v1 [mprefix, while        
    [38;2;177;185;249m/v1/submit [mand [38;2;177;185;249m/v1/permission [mdo. Hooks installed on a user's machine are long-lived, so the inconsistency becomes a
    compatibility problem the first time a payload changes.[61C
  - [1mLong-poll[22m [1mon[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/submit[m[1m.[22m With the halt design this is fine, but the doc should give the poll timeout and what a     
    client does on expiry. "Empty prompt" is called out as a failure elsewhere, so the empty-poll case needs a defined  
    response.[107C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mPATCH[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/tasks/{id} [m[1mcarries[22m [1mtoo[22m [1mmuch.[22m It sets overrides, status, why, rank, and [38;2;177;185;249mauto_approve[m. Status changes       
    trigger the lifecycle rules, and [38;2;177;185;249mauto_approve [mis a security-relevant switch. Splitting it, or at least documenting  
    which fields have side effects, would help reviewers.[63C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/events [m[1m(SSE)[22m [1mhas[22m [1mno[22m [1mstated[22m [1mresume[22m [1msemantics.[22m Does it honor [38;2;177;185;249mLast-Event-ID[m? After a reconnect, does the client    
    re-fetch state or replay? Over a mobile link through an overlay, reconnects will be common.[25C
  - [1mIdempotency[22m [1mis[22m [1monly[22m [1mdefined[22m [1mfor[22m [1mpermissions.[22m [38;2;177;185;249m/v1/submit [mwith [38;2;177;185;249mkind=task-complete [mretried after a timeout could       
    double-record. State whether it is safe to repeat.[66C
  - [38;2;177;185;249m[1mPOST[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/launch [m[1mand[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/harnesses [m[1maccept[22m [1mcommands.[22m Harness rows hold a command, arguments, and environment. Anyone  
    who can [38;2;177;185;249mPUT [ma harness and [38;2;177;185;249mPOST [ma launch can run arbitrary commands as the daemon's user. That is by design, but it  
    belongs in the threat model.[88C
[120C
  [1mSecurity[22m [1mand[22m [1mthe[22m [1mremote-access[22m [1mstory[22m[K[82C
[120C
  This is my largest concern. The doc says "no auth invented here" and relies on OpenZiti. That is defensible, but it   
  leaves a lot unsaid.[98C
[120C
  - [1mThe[22m [1mhuman[22m [1mAPI[22m [1mis[22m [1meffectively[22m [1mremote[22m [1mcode[22m [1mexecution.[22m A caller who can reach it can:[K[34C
    - approve any permission request,[83C
    - set [38;2;177;185;249mauto_approve[m,[K[97C
    - write standing rules,[93C
    - define harnesses and launch them,[81C
    - attach a terminal that accepts keystrokes,[72C
    - list the daemon's filesystem.[85C
[120C
    Access to the Ziti service is therefore equivalent to a shell on the daemon host. The doc should say that plainly   
    and describe how the service policy is scoped: which identities, and whether the agent-facing listener is ever      
    exposed.[108C
  - [1mOnly[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1m/v1/shutdown [m[1mhas[22m [1ma[22m [1mtoken.[22m It is "loopback only unless a token is configured." Everything else appears to depend
    on network position. If the human listener binds anywhere other than loopback without the overlay, there is no      
    authentication at all. A single bearer token on the whole human API would be cheap defense in depth.[16C
  - [1mBrowser[22m [1mattack[22m [1msurface[22m [1mon[22m [1mloopback.[22m A daemon listening on [38;2;177;185;249m127.0.0.1 [mwith no auth is reachable from any web page the 
    operator visits, through DNS rebinding or a simple cross-origin [38;2;177;185;249mPOST[m. The doc should require [38;2;177;185;249mHost [mheader checking   
    and [38;2;177;185;249mOrigin [mchecking on state-changing routes and on the WebSocket upgrade. WebSockets are not protected by the      
    same-origin policy, so [38;2;177;185;249m/attach [mis the highest-risk route here.[K[54C
  - [1mFail-open[22m [1mpermission[22m [1mhook.[22m The invariants section says the hook fails open when the daemon is unreachable or returns
    5xx. That is the right call for the "never burn tokens or block work" goal. It also means the halt, and any daemon  
    outage, turns off permission gating for every session. Stated in this document, that trade-off is a real security   
    decision and should be labeled as one. If an operator relies on [38;2;177;185;249mnever [mrules to stop destructive commands, a crashed 
    daemon silently removes them.[87C
  - [1mStanding[22m [1mrules[22m [1mimport.[22m Importing 134 rules from Claude Code's allow and deny lists is a productivity win. The doc   
    should say how a [38;2;177;185;249mdeny [min the source maps to a [38;2;177;185;249mnever [mhere, and what happens on a conflict between an imported allow  
    and an existing hand-written block.[81C
  - [1mRule[22m [1mspecificity.[22m "Prefix by default, glob when it contains [38;2;177;185;249m* [mor [38;2;177;185;249m?[m, most specific wins" needs a definition of       
    specific. Is [38;2;177;185;249mrm -f * [mmore specific than [38;2;177;185;249mrm[m? What happens on a tie between an allow and a deny? Deny-wins on ties is 
    the safe default and should be stated.[78C
  - [1mPath[22m [1mrules.[22m A folder rule needs canonicalization: symlinks, [38;2;177;185;249m..[m, case-insensitivity on Windows, and 8.3 short names. 
    The doc justifies path rules by the quoting problem, but it does not say how a path is normalized before matching.  
  - [1mAuto[22m [1mmode[22m [1msits[22m [1mlast[22m [1min[22m [1mthe[22m [1mchain.[22m That ordering is right, since [38;2;177;185;249mnever [mrules, shelved cards, and queued messages all 
    win. The deadline ([38;2;177;185;249mauto_until[m) is a good fix for the "left on forever" problem.[K[37C
[120C
  [1mSupervision[22m [1mand[22m [1mPTY[22m[K[99C
[120C
  - [1mThe[22m [1mexit-127[22m [1mtrap[22m is well documented, and recording it is valuable. The remedy (always [38;2;177;185;249mos.Exit [mexplicitly) would be 
    sturdier as a lint or a test that fails the build, since the symptom is silent.[37C
  - [1mScrubbing[22m [1mthe[22m [1menvironment[22m on launch is a subtle and correct decision. The failure (a child believing it is a nested 
    session and dropping transcripts) is exactly the kind of thing that costs an afternoon. I would keep the list of    
    scrubbed variables in one place and test it.[72C
  - [1mRing[22m [1mbuffer[22m [1mat[22m [1m16[22m [1mMB[22m [1mper[22m [1mtask.[22m Sixteen sessions cost 256 MB of resident memory in the daemon, and the doc allows    
    more. Consider a global cap, or lazy allocation for cards that have no attached terminal. The default was raised    
    because 256 KB was too small, which suggests the figure came from experience. Say what a "turn" costs in bytes so   
    the number can be reasoned about.[83C
  - [1mStop[22m [1msequence[22m (ctrl-c, then [38;2;177;185;249mexit[m, then close, then kill) is a sensible escalation. Each step needs a timeout, and   
    the document should list them.[86C
  - [1mDaemon[22m [1mrestart[22m [1mcosts[22m [1mevery[22m [1mterminal.[22m Because ConPTY has no reattach, any daemon restart ends every supervised       
    runner. The doc treats resume ids as the mitigation, which is fine, but it also means upgrades, crashes, and the    
    halt all have the same cost. A note on how often the daemon is expected to restart would help set expectations.     
  - [1mTyping[22m [1minto[22m [1ma[22m [1mshared[22m [1mterminal.[22m The peer bus rule (type when free, queue when not, never type into a half-typed line)
    depends on the daemon seeing every operator keystroke. That works only while every operator input goes through the  
    daemon. A local window-mode launch bypasses it, so say which modes support the rule.[32C
  - [1mLiveness[22m [1mby[22m [1mpid.[22m The reaper is cheap and correct in the common case. Pid reuse can keep a dead card looking alive if
    the OS hands the pid to another process within the sweep interval. Comparing the process start time with the        
    recorded launch time closes this. The doc already knows pids are recycled, so this belongs in the reaper's[10C
    description.[104C
[120C
  [1mFailure[22m [1mposture,[22m [1mfurther[22m [1mquestions[22m[K[84C
[120C
  - Halt state is in memory and on stderr only. That is right, but a supervisor restarting the process will lose the    
    cause. Writing a small sentinel file (not the database) would let the next start report why it stopped.[13C
  - "Refuse to restart out of it" means a halted daemon must be restarted by a human. If the daemon runs under a service
    manager with automatic restart, the process may come back, hit the same disk-full condition at open, and take tier  
    1 (refuse to start). That behaves acceptably, but it should be written down.[40C
  - Contention retry has "backoff" but no bound. An unbounded retry on a stuck lock never halts and never serves. A     
    ceiling that escalates to tier 3 is safer.[74C
  - The design treats [38;2;177;185;249mSQLITE_BUSY [mas normal. With a single daemon process and one writer, that should be rare. If it    
    happens often, something else is opening the file, and that deserves a log line.[36C
  - The transition from "daemon down" to "agents park" assumes every client uses the existing backoff. Non-cooperative  
    hooks ([38;2;177;185;249m/activity[m, [38;2;177;185;249m/session[m) fail silently by design. The doc covers this, and it is a good rule.[K[20C
[120C
  [1mStaging[22m [1mand[22m [1mprocess[22m[K[99C
[120C
  - The staged migration table reads as a status report. Marking stage 5 "Abandoned" while keeping it in the list is    
    honest, and it is useful history.[83C
  - Stage 8 is the weakest link. The doc says plainly that Ziti binding is "built, not proved." Given how much of goal 7
    and the security model rests on it, I would treat proving it as a blocker for calling v2 complete.[18C
  - The Postgres claim ("portable") is asserted, not tested, and the doc says so. Either add the CI job or drop[9C
    portability from the decisions table. An untested portability promise constrains the schema (no JSONB, text[9C
    timestamps) without delivering the benefit.[73C
  - Documentation cross-references ([38;2;177;185;249mactivity-design.md[m, [38;2;177;185;249mauto-mode.md[m, [38;2;177;185;249msupervision-design.md[m, [38;2;177;185;249mone-atrium-plan.md[m,        
    [38;2;177;185;249mtest-plan.md[m) are good practice. Confirm they exist and stay in sync, since this doc now defers to them.[K[12C
[120C
  [1mRecommendations[22m[K[103C
[120C
  Ordered by value for effort:[90C
[120C
  1. [1mAdd[22m [1ma[22m [1mthreat[22m [1mmodel[22m [1msection.[22m State that access to the human listener equals command execution on the host. List the 
     required checks: bearer token, [38;2;177;185;249mHost [mand [38;2;177;185;249mOrigin [mvalidation, and WebSocket origin checks. This is the highest-risk   
     gap.[111C
  2. [1mFix[22m [1mthe[22m [1mstale[22m [1mstatus[22m [1mline[22m [1mand[22m [1mthe[22m [1mcontradictions[22m in the table above. It takes minutes and restores trust in the    
     rest of the document.[94C
  3. [1mReconcile[22m [1mthe[22m [1mschema[22m [1mwith[22m [1mthe[22m [1mAPI.[22m Either regenerate the DDL block from migrations or list the columns added since.
  4. [1mDefine[22m [1mrule[22m [1mmatching[22m [1mprecisely:[22m specificity, tie-breaks (deny wins), and path normalization.[K[23C
  5. [1mDecide[22m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mON[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mDELETE[m [38;2;177;185;249m[1mCASCADE [m[1mdeliberately.[22m Forgetting a card should not silently erase its audit record unless that is 
     the intent, and then say so.[87C
  6. [1mDocument[22m [1mSSE[22m [1mresume[22m [1msemantics[22m and idempotency for [38;2;177;185;249msubmit [mand [38;2;177;185;249mtask-complete[m.[K[40C
  7. [1mProve[22m [1mthe[22m [1mZiti[22m [1mstage[22m [1mend[22m [1mto[22m [1mend[22m before treating remote access as a shipped feature.[K[32C
  8. [1mSplit[22m [1mthe[22m [1mdocument[22m into "architecture as built" and "history and abandoned ideas."[K[33C
[120C
  [1mClosing[22m [1massessment[22m[K[100C
[120C
  The core bet is right: a durable task model with declared status, fronted by a small JSON and SSE API. The document's 
  habit of recording why each rule exists (the trap, the failure, the alternative rejected) is its best quality, and I  
  would keep it. The remaining work is mostly hygiene and one substantial gap: the security posture of a remote,        
  unauthenticated, command-capable control plane. Fix that gap first.[51C
[120C
